    <%# == One unstable test, opened
        The rung BELOW the panel above, and the end of that ladder. Every ranking here is a
        capped ten and this one is an AGGREGATE besides — `SpecObservation::UNSTABLE_COMPOSITION` is
        every column a `COUNT` or an `ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT …)` under `GROUP BY name`, which is what
        keeps it constant in the size of the suite and is exactly what discards the run axis. A
        reader who found their flakiest test up there had found the end of the road.

        WHAT THE PANEL ABOVE CANNOT SAY, and the whole reason this exists. A row up there reads
        `30 runs, 4 failed, [failed, passed]`, and those three figures are IDENTICAL for two windows
        that call for opposite work: four failures at runs 27–30 is a REGRESSION — find the commit
        between run 26 and run 27 — and four failures at runs 3, 11, 19 and 26 is FLAKINESS, where
        there is no culprit commit and the work is quarantine or shared state. Nor is the sequence
        derivable from anything else this page serves: the Recent runs panel has no per-test grain
        at all, and every per-example list above it carries an outcome for ONE run.

        THE SAME WINDOW as the panel above and the chart above that — literally the same loaded runs
        (see `RepositoriesController#show`), which here is not merely economy. These rows are read
        for their POSITION against commits the panels above already printed, so a separately fetched
        window would put an off-by-one between the sequence and the commits it is read against, and
        naming the wrong culprit commit is worse than naming none.

        Rendered only when a test was ASKED for, and the read is taken only then: a page nobody
        asked a test of costs exactly what it cost before this existed. NOT gated on the ranking
        above being comparable — a window that ranking has nothing to say about is precisely where
        the raw per-run grain is worth having, and gating this on that would withhold the grain
        exactly when the aggregate went silent.

        Same visibility rule and same gate as the panels above: read-only suite telemetry on
        `current_repository(:view)`, outside `manage_keys`, and nothing here widens that. %>
    <% if @unstable_test_runs %>
      <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "This test, run by run", id: "unstable-test-runs",
                                        class: "lg:col-span-3") do |panel| %>
        <% panel.with_actions do %>
          <%# The way back OUT, for the reason the other "Close" controls exist: this panel is
              reached by a link and there is no other control that removes the parameter, so without
              this the only exit is editing the URL — and the reader who most needs the drill-in is
              the one least likely to want to do that. It anchors back at the ranking the test was
              picked from, so closing a test returns the reader where they opened it rather than at
              the top of a long page.

              Through `drill_down_path`, with `unstable_test: nil` naming the one ask it clears, and
              explicit for the same reason every clearing gesture on this page is. Carry is the
              default, so an omission here would mean "keep the test open" and this button would
              navigate to the page it is already on. Every OTHER ask rides through, so closing a
              test does not close a file, an area or a description the reader opened separately, nor
              jump the page back to the newest run. %>
          <%= render UI::ButtonComponent.new(
                variant: :ghost,
                href: drill_down_path(@repository, unstable_test: nil, anchor: "unstable-tests")
              ) { "Close test" } %>
        <% end %>
        <% if @unstable_test_runs.rows.any? %>
          <%# THE CAP, IN FRONT OF THE TABLE RATHER THAN INSIDE THE PARAGRAPH BELOW IT — the one
              disclosure on this panel that is not a footnote.

              Every sibling drill-in discloses its cap in its scope sentence and is right to: those
              lists are read for WHAT IS IN THEM, so a reader holding the top 200 of 250 rows holds
              the answer they came for. This list is read for WHERE IN THE SEQUENCE the failures
              sit, and this window runs oldest first, so the cap removes the newest end — which is
              the end the regression reading is taken from. Truncation here does not shorten the
              answer, it removes it, and it does so while leaving a full-looking 200-row table
              behind. A missing answer that renders identically to a negative one is precisely what
              a reader cannot detect for themselves, so it goes in front of the prose rather than
              inside it.

              Rendered only when the cap BIT, through the same `#unstable_test_runs_capped?` the
              scope sentence and the reading rule branch on, so the page cannot show this alert over
              a sentence saying the list is complete or hide it over one saying it is not. %>
          <% if unstable_test_runs_capped?(@unstable_test_runs) %>
            <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :warning, class: "mb-3",
                                              id: "unstable-test-runs-cap",
                                              title: unstable_test_runs_cap_alert_title) do %>
              <p class="mt-1"><%= unstable_test_runs_cap_alert_body(@unstable_test_runs) %></p>
            <% end %>
          <% end %>
          <%# What the list IS and HOW TO READ IT, before the list — the second half being this
              panel's own and not a discipline it inherits. Its siblings' captions size a list; this
              one also states the reading, because the shape of the column IS the finding and a
              reader who has the sequence without the rule reads it and guesses.

              Every figure is counted off THIS DESCRIPTION'S ROWS in the same read that returned the
              list — `SpecObservation::UNSTABLE_TEST_RUN_POPULATION_COUNTS` is two windows on that
              one query — so the caption cannot end up describing a different population from the
              rows under it. %>
          <p class="text-xs text-app-muted mb-3" id="unstable-test-runs-basis">
            <span class="text-app-content-secondary"><%= @unstable_test_runs.name %></span>
            — <%= unstable_test_runs_scope_sentence(@unstable_test_runs) %>
            <%# The rule for reading the column, HANDED THE SEQUENCE because which readings are
                available depends on whether the cap took the end they are read from. It withholds
                the regression branch when it did — see `RepositoriesHelper`. %>
            <%= unstable_test_runs_reading_sentence(@unstable_test_runs) %>
            <%# The silence, rendered only when the window holds any: rows that recorded the test and
                reported no outcome are not passes, and a sequence that let them read as one would
                manufacture a flip that looks like a date. %>
            <%= unstable_test_runs_silence_clause(@unstable_test_runs) %>
            <%# One row per run is what the data USUALLY is rather than what this list promises, and
                it comes apart in both directions. Said here rather than left to a reader counting
                rows against the window length in the sentence above them — which is exactly the
                subtraction that would invent a run that "went missing". %>
            A run that recorded nothing under this description contributes no row, and a description
            carried by more than one example in a run contributes one row per example — so the
            length of this list is not the length of the window, and which run a row belongs to is
            read off its commit rather than off its position.
            <%# The two per-run columns that are read ACROSS rows, and the reading each of them needs
                before it can be read that way. Static rather than counted off this window, like the
                reading rule above it and unlike the silence clause beside it: both are rules that
                hold of every sequence this panel draws, and neither states a figure about these
                rows, so there is no population here for a sentence to disagree with. %>
            Durations are the client's own per-example timings and not anything measured here, and a
            run whose client reported none says so rather than reading as a zero. A definition site
            that CHANGES down this list is the identity rule working — a test that moved is the same
            test and keeps its history — and not two different tests sharing a description; each row
            links against its own run's commit, so two rows pointing at two different files can both
            be right.
          </p>
          <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(
                columns: ["Commit", "Definition site", "Branch", "Ingested", "Duration", "Outcome"],
                describedby: "unstable-test-runs-basis"
              ) do %>
            <% @unstable_test_runs.rows.each do |row| %>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">
                  <%# The join a reader takes this panel for: the commit is what they open next, and
                      on a regression the one to look at is the commit of the first failing row.
                      Seven characters, the same abbreviation the Recent runs panel below uses, so
                      one page renders one sha one way. %>
                  <%= row.commit_sha.first(7) %>
                </td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content-secondary">
                  <%# WHERE THE TEST WAS DEFINED IN THIS RUN, PINNED TO THIS ROW'S OWN COMMIT — the
                      one thing this panel can say that its four sibling per-example listings
                      cannot. They each list ONE run's rows, so they pin every link to the
                      page-level `@latest_test_run.commit_sha` and are right to. These rows each
                      carry their own run (`Row#run`, `#commit_sha`), and under the project's
                      semantic identity rule a test that MOVED is the same test and keeps its
                      history — so a coordinate is allowed to change down this list, and the only
                      honest ref for a row is the commit that actually ran that example. Borrowing
                      the page's sha here would point a reader at whatever now sits on that line of
                      a file that need not have held this test at the time, which is the
                      wrong-culprit reading in the file dimension.

                      Built from `file_path`, NEVER from `spec_file_path` — the rule
                      `SpecObservation#location_label` and `Repository#github_blob_url`'s `@param
                      path` both state. `spec_file_path` is the *including* file and differs from
                      `file_path` only for a shared example group, which is exactly the case where
                      the line number describes a line in the OTHER file: pairing them prints a
                      coordinate whose two halves come from two files and links at whatever happens
                      to sit on that line of the including one. `Row` exposes `spec_file_path` and
                      not `file_path`, so this reads through `row.observation` — the same reach the
                      outcome cell below already makes, and the one that needs no model change.

                      ZERO NEW QUERIES: `SpecObservation.outcome_sequence_in` selects
                      `spec_observations.*`, so both halves are already loaded; `@repository` is
                      loaded long before this partial renders; and `#github_blob_url` is string
                      composition.

                      Rendered only when the coordinate has BOTH halves, and rendered as NOTHING
                      when it does not. `file_path` and `line_number` are `NOT NULL` and
                      `Ingest::Payload` rejects a delivery missing either, so this guards a pair
                      that should never come apart rather than a branch anyone expects to fire —
                      but the two shapes it forecloses are both worse than an empty cell: a bare
                      ":" reads as a coordinate, and `…/blob/<sha>/#L1` resolves to the repository
                      root, so a broken link here would LOOK like it worked. %>
                  <% if row.observation.file_path.present? && row.observation.line_number.present? %>
                    <%= link_to row.observation.location_label,
                                @repository.github_blob_url(row.observation.file_path,
                                                            row.observation.line_number,
                                                            row.commit_sha),
                                class: "underline underline-offset-2 decoration-app-border-light " \
                                       "text-app-content hover:decoration-app-content",
                                target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer" %>
                  <% end %>
                </td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
                  <%# Per-row rather than borrowed from the window, on the rule
                      `Api::V1::RepositoriesController#serialized_history_row` states for the same
                      field: a row should carry its own branch so it is readable on its own. Every
                      row here carries the same value — the window is branch-scoped by construction
                      — and `branch` is nullable in the schema besides, so a blank cell would be a
                      client omission dressed up as a fact. %>
                  <%= row.branch.presence || tag.span("not reported", class: "text-app-muted") %>
                </td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary tabular-nums">
                  <%# WHEN, which is also what separates two rows of ONE commit. A commit is not a
                      key — the same commit is legitimately ingested more than once, as a re-run, a
                      retry or a second workflow — so a reader deciding that a failure MOVED needs
                      to be able to tell two runs of one sha apart before they go looking at it. %>
                  <%= time_ago_in_words(row.ingested_at) %> ago
                </td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary tabular-nums">
                  <%# HOW LONG THIS RUN TOOK OVER THIS EXAMPLE — the column that separates the two
                      readings the outcome column can only get as far as. Once the sequence has said
                      "scattered", the reader's next question is WHY, and for the largest class of
                      real flakes the answer is timing: a test that passes at 4s and fails at 31s is
                      hitting a timeout rather than behaving randomly, and nothing but the per-run
                      grain can show that.

                      Through `#duration_label`, the model's one formatting seam for a figure at
                      this table's grain, and deliberately not re-formatted here. Its first line is
                      `return "not reported" if seconds.nil?`, and its own doc comment names the
                      hazard that line exists for: `duration_seconds` is nullable — `result&.run_time`
                      comes back nil for an example that never ran — so an ungated nil formats as
                      "0.00s" and makes "the client sent no timing" byte-identical to "this test
                      took no time". On a column read for a timeout, that is the one wrong answer it
                      must not produce.

                      `tabular-nums` like the Ingested cell beside it: this column is read by
                      comparing rows DOWN it rather than one row at a time, and proportional digits
                      put the same magnitudes at different widths. %>
                  <%= row.observation.duration_label %>
                </td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2">
                  <%# What that run said happened, through `#outcome_label` and `#outcome_tone` — the
                      same pair every outcome on this page renders through, so a word cannot be
                      coloured one way in a run's list and another way down a window. Echoed verbatim
                      and never reworded into a verdict: nothing platform-side validates this string,
                      so quoting what arrived is the only reading that cannot be wrong, and a run
                      that reported none reads "not reported" rather than wearing the spelling of a
                      pass.

                      Read off the row's OBSERVATION rather than re-spelled from `Row#outcome` here:
                      `#outcome_label` and `#outcome_tone` are the model's own seam and a second
                      `outcome.presence || "not reported"` in this file would be a second place for
                      one rendering to drift. %>
                  <%= render UI::BadgeComponent.new(tone: row.observation.outcome_tone) do %>
                    <%= row.observation.outcome_label %>
                  <% end %>
                </td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          <% end %>
        <% else %>
          <%# The window recorded nothing under the description that was asked for. An ordinary
              answer and not an error, on the spelling the sibling drill-ins fixed: `?unstable_test=`
              is a URL a reader types, edits and bookmarks, and the commonest way to arrive here is
              the identity rule working as designed — a renamed test starts a new history and its old
              one stops at the rename. No 404, and the description is named back, because an empty
              state without a subject is a sentence about nothing. %>
          <div id="unstable-test-runs-none">
            <%= render UI::EmptyStateComponent.new(
                  title: "No runs recorded this description",
                  description: unstable_test_runs_none_description(@unstable_test_runs)
                ) %>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
